WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.897
Bounded copying and concatenation APIs are
designed to make buffer limits visible,

2
00:00:04.977 --> 00:00:07.454
but they still depend on accurate
information.

3
00:00:07.534 --> 00:00:11.125
The destination capacity must describe the
actual writable object.

4
00:00:11.205 --> 00:00:13.434
The source must meet the API's input
rules.

5
00:00:13.514 --> 00:00:17.601
The caller must know whether the result
was complete, truncated, or rejected.

6
00:00:17.681 --> 00:00:22.078
Concatenation adds another requirement:
the current destination length matters.

7
00:00:22.158 --> 00:00:26.493
A buffer that has enough total capacity
for one string may not have enough remaining

8
00:00:26.573 --> 00:00:27.811
capacity for an append.

9
00:00:27.891 --> 00:00:30.492
If the existing destination is not a valid
string,

10
00:00:30.572 --> 00:00:33.296
an append-style API may start from an
invalid state.

11
00:00:33.376 --> 00:00:35.853
Terminator space is part of capacity
planning.

12
00:00:35.933 --> 00:00:40.082
A copied or concatenated C string usually
needs storage for the content plus the

13
00:00:40.162 --> 00:00:41.091
null terminator.

14
00:00:41.171 --> 00:00:45.444
The code should make it obvious whether
the capacity includes that terminator and

15
00:00:45.524 --> 00:00:48.558
what happens when there is no room for the
complete result.

16
00:00:48.638 --> 00:00:51.487
Truncation semantics vary across APIs and
libraries.

17
00:00:51.567 --> 00:00:54.725
In some contexts, truncated display text
may be acceptable.

18
00:00:54.805 --> 00:00:58.458
In configuration values, identifiers, file
paths, protocol fields,

19
00:00:58.538 --> 00:01:02.873
or authorization-related labels,
truncation may change meaning and should cause

20
00:01:02.953 --> 00:01:04.191
rejection or recovery.

21
00:01:04.271 --> 00:01:06.315
The application, not the library name,

22
00:01:06.395 --> 00:01:08.872
decides whether a partial result is safe
to use.

23
00:01:08.952 --> 00:01:10.933
Checked results are non-negotiable.

24
00:01:11.013 --> 00:01:15.596
Bounded APIs often communicate required
length, bytes copied, status, or truncation.

25
00:01:15.676 --> 00:01:20.072
Ignoring that result can make the code
look safer while still using incomplete data.

26
00:01:20.152 --> 00:01:23.620
Reviewers should ask what the code does
when the output did not fit.
